---
import Adr from "../../components/Adr.astro";
import Layer from "../../components/Layer.astro";
import Layout from "../../components/Layout.astro";
import PageLink from "../../components/PageLink.astro";
import Path from "../../components/Path.astro";
import Routes from "../../components/Routes.astro";
import Rule from "../../components/Rule.astro";

const M = "server/src/iki_server/runs/models.py";
const SV = "server/src/iki_server/runs/services.py";
const Q = "server/src/iki_server/runs/queue.py";
const AP = "server/src/iki_server/runs/approvals.py";

const H = {
  short: "What a launch does",
  how: "The checks, in order",
  rules: "Projects, reuse, batches and approval",
  ref: "Routes, code and decisions",
};
const toc = [
  { id: "short", label: H.short },
  { id: "how", label: H.how },
  { id: "writes", label: "What it writes" },
  { id: "rules", label: H.rules },
  { id: "ref", label: H.ref },
];
---

<Layout toc={toc}>
  <Layer n={1} title={H.short}>
    <p>
      A launch checks everything that could make a run fail for a reason other than its own work,
      so a worker never starts something that can't be committed. Then, in one transaction, it
      decides whether an identical earlier run's results can be reused, checks budgets, consent,
      approval and the queued quota, and writes the <code>ActionRun</code>, its pinned inputs and
      its first <code>Job</code>. Once that commits, <code>pg_notify</code> wakes the tier's workers
      (<Adr n="0042" />, <Path p={SV} sym="def launch(" />).
    </p>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={2} title={H.how}>
    <p>Before the transaction, each refusal names what's wrong:</p>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <strong>The tier and the deployment.</strong> The caller has the tier's permission in the
        project (Editor for self-hosted and backend, Runner for container and vm, edit for the{" "}
        <code>server</code> tier), the project isn't archived, and the action can run here: its
        plugin isn't detached, its required settings and secrets are set, the egress proxy is on if
        it declares hosts, its outside APIs are usable, and its environment is bound to a template
        on its tier (<Path p={SV} sym="def may_launch(" />, <Path p={Q} sym="TIER_ACTIONS = {" />,{" "}
        <Path p="server/src/iki_server/runs/catalog.py" sym="def availability(" />).
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Labels</strong>, only for a self-hosted run (<Adr n="0112" />, <Path
          p={SV}
          sym="def launch_labels("
        />).
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Inputs.</strong> Each is a record the caller can read, of the input's type, not
        archived. One the action updates must be editable as the run's project, not another side's
        fields of a request, not locked, and needs a reason if a lock on it was lifted (<Path
          p={SV}
          sym="def check_inputs("
        />).
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Params</strong> fit their fields; a file param is one of the project's files (<Path
          p={SV}
          sym="def check_params("
        />).
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Included references.</strong> What each input's <code>include</code> names is
        resolved now; one the caller can't read refuses the launch (<Adr n="0105" />, <Path
          p="server/src/iki_server/runs/included.py"
          sym="def resolve("
        />).
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The project.</strong> Every input, and everything its references name, is in the
        run's project (see below).
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Outside APIs</strong> the action calls are approved in the project (<PageLink
          to="Network and secrets"
        />).
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>Then, inside one transaction:</p>
    <ol>
      <li>Outside inputs are linked into the project, if the caller asked.</li>
      <li>
        The setup builds of the environment's volumes are pinned, the reuse key is made, and an
        identical run is looked for. If one is found, the new run ends here (<Path
          p={SV}
          sym="def _made("
        />).
      </li>
      <li>Enforced budgets (<PageLink to="Quotas and cost" />).</li>
      <li>Consent to what the action sends to outside APIs (<Adr n="0130" />).</li>
      <li>Approval, for an unattended costly run (below).</li>
      <li>The quota on waiting runs, unless the run waits for approval.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3 id="writes">What it writes</h3>
    <p>
      <code>_queue</code> creates the <code>ActionRun</code>, which pins what the run will read and
      run (the plugin commit, the action as installed, params, settings, setup builds, an image if
      one is bound, a seed); a <code>RunInput</code> for each input at its revision and a{" "}
      <code>RunInclusion</code> for each record its references name, both append-only; and one{" "}
      <code>Job</code> with the tier, environment, labels and resources (the environment's, raised to
      what the action asks). <PageLink to="Pinning and provenance" /> says what each pin is for.
      The job is <code>queued</code>, or <code>awaiting_approval</code>, where no worker leases it
      (<Path p={SV} sym="def _queue(" />, <Path p={M} sym="class Job(" />).
    </p>
    <p>
      <code>queue.notify</code> sends <code>pg_notify</code> on the <code>iki_jobs</code> channel
      with the tier's name, on commit, so a worker never wakes for a job it can't see yet (<Path
        p={Q}
        sym="def notify("
      />). How the web processes listen is on <PageLink to="Tasks and live updates" />. The launch is
      audited as <code>run.launched</code>.
    </p>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={4} title={H.rules}>
    <div class="rules">
      <Rule title="A run reads only what's in its project" open>
        <p>
          Every input, and everything its included references name, must be in the run's project;
          otherwise the launch is refused with 409 <code>inputs_outside_project</code>, unless the
          caller asks to link them in first, which is audited as the link API's links are. One whose
          project doesn't allow sharing can't be linked. Reproduction refuses if an input has since
          left (<Adr n={[66, 67, 105]} />, <Path p={SV} sym="def outside_project(" />). How links
          work is on <PageLink to="Sharing across projects" />.
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="Reuse is decided at launch">
        <p>
          A deterministic run's reuse key is made in the launch's transaction, before budgets,
          consent and approval. If a run with the same key succeeded, the new run is written{" "}
          <code>succeeded</code> at once, naming it, with no job, and needs no budget, approval or
          quota. A run whose volumes have no ready build yet isn't matched: it waits, and the
          provisioner pins the first ready build and completes its key (<Path
            p={SV}
            sym="def pin_waiting("
          />). What the key covers is on <PageLink to="Pinning and provenance" />.
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="Batches">
        <p>
          <code>launch_each</code> launches one run for each record given as one input, with the
          others shared, or none at all. They share a <code>RunBatch</code> and one approval; runs
          that reuse results need none. The API takes up to 1000 records at once, the web app 200
          (<Adr n="0055" />, <Path p={SV} sym="def launch_each(" />, <Path
            p="server/src/iki_server/api/runs.py"
            sym="MAX_BATCH = 1000"
          />). Packing a batch's runs into one job happens at lease time (<PageLink
            to="The worker and its leases"
          />).
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="Unattended costly runs wait for their person">
        <p>
          A run launched through MCP or by a trigger waits for approval when it's on the container or
          vm tier, or calls outside APIs. One request is one decision, even for a batch. An OAuth
          token can't approve, so an agent can't approve its own request; an ask nobody decides
          within 7 days expires, and the run ends <code>declined</code>. Approving checks again that
          the person may run the tier, the budgets, the outside APIs' approval and the queued quota,
          and for an action that sends data out it's the person's consent (<Adr
            n={[13, 50, 130]}
          />, <Path p={AP} sym="def needed(" />, <Path p={AP} sym="def approve(" />, <Path
            p={AP}
            sym="EXPIRES_AFTER = timedelta(days=7)"
          />).
        </p>
      </Rule>
    </div>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={5} title={H.ref}>
    <h3>Routes</h3>
    <Routes paths={/^\/api\/v1\/(actions|batches|run-approvals)/} />
    <div class="refs">
      <div>
        <h3>Where</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><Path p={SV} /></li>
          <li><Path p="server/src/iki_server/runs/included.py" /></li>
          <li><Path p={AP} /></li>
          <li><Path p="server/src/iki_server/runs/catalog.py" /></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>Decisions</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><Adr n="0042" title /></li>
          <li><Adr n="0050" title /></li>
          <li><Adr n="0055" title /></li>
          <li><Adr n="0066" title /></li>
          <li><Adr n="0105" title /></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>Where the ADRs and code differ</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><Adr n="0042" /> caps a batch at 100 runs; <Adr n="0055" /> and the code (<code>MAX_BATCH = 1000</code>) say 1000.</li>
          <li><Adr n="0050" /> holds only MCP runs for approval; the code also holds triggers' runs, and runs that call outside APIs on any tier.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </Layer>
</Layout>
